Use a compiled timeline + 3-mesh render split. It keeps logic clean and avoids per-layer duplication.

**1) Per-segment lifecycle model**
```ts
type SegmentId = number;
type LayerIndex = number;
type Vec2 = readonly [number, number];
type Vec3 = readonly [number, number, number];

type LegMode = "pass" | "settle";

interface SegmentDef {
  id: SegmentId;
  size: Vec2;
  uvRect: readonly [number, number, number, number];
  originXY: Vec2;
}

interface SwapLayer {
  layer: LayerIndex; // 1..L
  pairs: readonly [SegmentId, SegmentId][];
}

interface SegmentLeg {
  segmentId: SegmentId;
  layer: LayerIndex;      // leg is (layer-1) -> layer
  mode: LegMode;
  from: Vec3;
  to: Vec3;
  cp1: Vec3;              // bezier control points for ConnectionLine
  cp2: Vec3;
}

interface SegmentLifecycle {
  segmentId: SegmentId;
  settleLayer: LayerIndex; // first layer where it is swapped
  legs: SegmentLeg[];      // 1..settleLayer
}

interface CompiledPlan {
  layers: number;
  lifecycles: SegmentLifecycle[];
  legsByLayer: SegmentLeg[][];     // indexed by layer
  settleIdsByLayer: SegmentId[][];
  xyByLayer: Vec2[][];             // [layer][segmentId]
}
```

Build `xyByLayer` from swaps, then derive each segment’s `settleLayer` and legs. This directly models “pass 1..5, settle 6”.

**2) Single mesh vs per-layer meshes**
Recommended: **3 instanced meshes**, not per-layer meshes.

- `baseMesh` (static): layer 0 snapshot, all segments.
- `activeMesh` (dynamic): one instance per unsettled segment currently flying/waiting.
- `settledMesh` (append-only): segments that have settled, persistent at their settle layer.

This is simpler than per-layer meshes and still very fast at ~33 segments.

